# Miso (Children of Morta: Tactics, codename Miso)

Turn-based tactical RPG spin-off in the Children of Morta universe. Unity 6000.3.4f1, C#, namespace `MISO...`. Repo root `E:\Work\Projects\miso`, Unity project in `miso\`, code under `miso\Assets\_Project\`. Working branch: `thj/home-test`. Main branch: `main` (remote `origin/main`).

I am THJ (designer/dev). The Home system (the between-missions house, days, stations, Bond, Picnic, Great Moon, Warren) lives in `miso\Assets\_Project\Core\Systems\HomeSystem\{Runtime,Editor}`.

The root `README.md` is untouched GitLab boilerplate; ignore it.

## Notes in `claude-handoff/` (repo root, excluded from git)

Notes, not instructions: if one conflicts with this file or with what I tell you, this file and I win. Read the one you need, not all of them.

- `home-system-handoff.md`: current state of the Home system, known issues, open decisions. Start here for Home work.
- `Miso-Home-System_Demo-Spec.md`: what the demo should do (rules and numbers). Copy of the Demo Spec tab.
- `Miso-Home-System_M9-Readability.md`: the M9 design. Copy of its tab.
- `Playtest-Plan.md`: the Play checks still to run, and the cold-playtest protocol.
- `Build-Log_additions.md`: where you write new Build Log sections (see below).
- `miso-mechanics-brief.md`: how the combat code works. Read before touching `Core/`. Written 2026-10-03; later merges of main are not in it.
- `player-skill-trees.md`, `combat-balance.md`, `combat-log.md`, `reward-balance.md` (with `reward-tools/`): design and code notes for those areas.
- `ai-regression-test-triage.md`: why `AIRegressionTest.Baseline_MatchesCurrentAI` is red and must stay red.

The masters of the Demo Spec, the M9 design and the full Build Log are the tabs of the "Miso Home System" doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/9Fi2AJKcLbf1e7evrkMxXN); the local files are copies. When you finish a step, append a dated section to `Build-Log_additions.md` (Built / Verified / Not verified / Findings / Status) and tell me; it gets folded into the Build Log tab. Change the Demo Spec only when a game rule or number changes.

## How to work with me

- For new builds, systems and features: review the code, propose the design, flag conflicts, then wait for my answer before building. Show me the plan first for risky steps (merges, scene or prefab resolution, shared Core code).
- Keep me posted with short status messages during long work.
- Add edit-mode tests for logic and run the EditMode suite after code changes. Every test must pass except `AIRegressionTest.Baseline_MatchesCurrentAI`, which is known red; do not "fix" it.

## Git rules

- Read-only git is always fine (`status`, `diff`, `log`, `show`, `branch`, `merge-base`, `ls-files`, ...). Scope paths to a folder on this large repo.
- Tell me before any commit, merge, rebase, checkout that changes files, reset, stash, push or fetch, then wait for my yes (they use my git identity). Before a commit, show me `git status`, `git diff --stat` and the files you plan to stage. Never stage everything blindly (Unity regenerates `.meta` files and scene noise).
- Never push. Never force anything. Never delete branches.
- Keep each file's line endings and BOM (many files are CRLF; the Home files are LF; `CameraHandler.cs` and `CheatHandler.cs` have a BOM).
- Close Unity before a merge (or tell me to); after it, open Unity, recompile (Ctrl+R), run the tests.
- `CLAUDE.md` and `claude-handoff/` are in `.git/info/exclude` on purpose. Never stage or commit them.

## Hard rules

- Ask me before resetting the game save.
- Domain reload is OFF: every new `static` needs a `PlayModeStaticReset` hook. Add a `ResetPlayModeState()` method on the class and call it from `Reset()` in `Core/Managers/PlayModeStaticReset.cs` (`HomeState.ResetPlayModeState()` is an example).
- Stop Play mode before editing scripts.
- Locale bug: `ToString("P0")` gives "30 %"; format percents by hand.
- Prefer new partial-class files over editing big ones.

## Home system essentials

- Pure C# `HomeSimulation` (no Unity types) driven by the scene MonoBehaviour `HomeDayRunner`; ScriptableObject definitions with GUID ids; demo content built by `Tools/Home/Build Demo Content` (`HomeDemoContentBuilder.cs`).
- Folders under `Core/Systems/HomeSystem/`:
  - `Runtime/Simulation`, `Runtime/State`: pure C#, no Unity types.
  - `Runtime/Definitions`: ScriptableObjects with GUID ids.
  - `Runtime/Presentation`, `Runtime/UI`: the scene views.
  - `Runtime/Combat`: the bridge into sorties (`HomeSortieBridge`, the Omen, SendOff and Dungeon appliers, From Home tags, Bond locks, Fireside payoff).
  - `Editor/`: the idempotent builders and the tests.
- Builders (Tools/Home menu, Play off), in order: Build Demo Content, Setup Home Scene, Build Presentation UI, Build House View, Camera/Apply Boundary Fit (optional: Build Stat Layers). One-time tools: Add Combat Tags, Build House Traces, Add Bond Locks (re-enter Play afterwards).
- `Core/Home/Runtime/` holds only the small idle-list `HomeStateManager` / `HomeState`. Two `HomeState` types exist: `MISO.Core.Home.HomeState` (idle lists) and the Home system's `Runtime/State/HomeState.cs` (simulation state).
- Home scene: `miso\Assets\_Project\Features\Home - Imported\___Khashayar\Home - Scene.unity`; most of what the Home system adds to it comes from the builders.
- Day slots Morning / Afternoon / Dusk / Night. The Warren dungeon opens only on full-moon Day 7. The Picnic (Hold / Skip) comes before the end-of-demo Win screen. Bond locks (`HomeLockRules`) and staged station unlocks (the cycle's `stagedUnlocks`) gate abilities and rooms.
- Sim Debug window: `Tools/Home/Sim Debug` (live mode attaches to the scene's sim).
- Unplaced text is tagged `[PLACEHOLDER] ` in data; `HomeText.Display` strips the tag on screen.
- Dev cheats in combat (`Core/_Dev_/Runtime/CheatHandler.cs`): K kills the hovered unit, F11 on a hovered enemy kills its whole faction, F12 completes the objective, H heal, J ATB, L +99 family points, `[` −1 HP (Shift −10), `]` +1 AP. Sim Debug test tools: "+5 (test)" Bond per pair, "Grant +5" materials, "Grant study item", "Grant dish (test)", "Unlock the Picnic (test)".

## Combat architecture (details in `miso-mechanics-brief.md`)

- **Subsystems** under `Core/Systems/`: `AbilitySystem` (Abilities, Attributes, Effects, Points), `AISystem`, `GridSystem`, `StatsResourcesSystem`, `RunUpgrades`, `TagSystem`, `CombatLogging`. Level and mission flow in `Core/Managers` and `Core/Levels`; units in `Core/Units` (`GUnit`, both sides); player and enemy content in `Features/{Player,Enemy}`.
- **Turn flow:** `CombatLevelRunHandler` (Core/Managers) runs one `FactionControllerBase` per faction (Core/Interactions). Each controller is a per-unit function-object FSM: `UnitSelection -> ActionSelection -> TargetSelection -> TakeAction`. `PlayerController` and `AIController` drive the same FSM.
- **AI** is utility-based (blackboard, strategy planner, per-ability score graphs) and runs on a background thread (`AIWorker`). Code the AI reads (`GridCell`, `GUnit`, stat code) must not use Unity's overloaded `==` or main-thread-only APIs; use `ReferenceEquals` plus `IsAlive`, and `GameEntityMessage` instead of `SendMessage`. `GridCell` occupancy depends on `IsAlive`, so any despawn path must clear `IsAlive` or the cell slot. Changing a utility graph, AI ability or unit also changes `AIRegressionTest`'s baked catalogs (already red).
- **Abilities:** `AbilityPreset` is authored data, `RuntimeAbility` the live instance, `AbilityOrchestrator` the per-unit container. Effects are `AEffect*` classes (`AEffectCompound` composes sub-effects). Duos are data entries plus hardcoded per-duo hooks in `UnitsCooperationHandler`, so a new duo needs code.
- **Points:** AP goes through `APConsumer` (`PerUnit` or `Shared` from `GlobalSettings`). Faction-role and duo spending goes through `PointConsumer` (FTP or ATB per `pointConsumptionMode`). This branch uses FTP; `origin/main` uses ATB. The Reactive Interrupt (key L) works only in ATB mode.
- **Stats:** raw `AAttribute` pools (fixed modifier order) plus evaluated `StatAttributeSet` factors. Source order: Ability, Unit, Equipment, Faction, SkillTree (plus `Home` when present), in `StatEvaluation.cs`.
- **Run upgrades:** one mission is one run. `RunUpgradeTracker` (on `FactionTracker`) is in memory only (as are `MissionTracker.GraphSeed` and `CompletedLevelNames`) and re-applies grants to recreated units each level via `PrepareLevel`.
- **Missions** are level graphs (`SerializableDependencyGraph`, seeded by `MissionTracker.GraphSeed`); the in-mission picker is `MissionMapUI`, the world-map picker is separate.
- **Naming:** the unit Dom was renamed Toba, but its folder is still `Features/Player/Dom/`. Static save trackers (`EnemyIntroductionTracker`, `AbilityIntroductionTracker`, `PlayedSequencesTracker`, `BarkPlayedTracker`) are static `ISavable` singletons that call `SaveLoadManager.Register` before every access.

## Build & test

- No asmdefs under `Assets/_Project`: game code compiles into `Assembly-CSharp`, editor code and tests into `Assembly-CSharp-Editor`. Edit-mode tests sit in `Editor/` folders. Home tests: `Core/Systems/HomeSystem/Editor/HomeSimulationTests.<Area>.cs`, one partial file per area (namespace `MISO.Core.Systems.HomeSystem.EditorTests`); put new Home tests in a new partial.
- Normal route: Window > General > Test Runner > EditMode > Run All.
- Command line (only with the editor CLOSED; Unity 6000.3.5f2 is also installed, so use the 6000.3.4f1 path):
  "C:/Program Files/Unity/Hub/Editor/6000.3.4f1/Editor/Unity.exe" -batchmode -projectPath "E:/Work/Projects/miso/miso" -runTests -testPlatform EditMode -testResults "<scratch>/results.xml" -logFile "<scratch>/test.log"
  Add `-testFilter "MISO.Core.Systems.HomeSystem.EditorTests.HomeSimulationTests"` (or a full test name) to run a subset.
